        <!-- trunk_cam: direct user request -- mounted right above where the
             trunk itself attaches (trunk_mount sits at head-local
             0.09,0,-0.02), dead centre, y=0 -- NOT outboard and NOT verged
             like eye_left/eye_right. Centring the object in THIS camera's
             frame is, by construction, centring it on the trunk's own reach
             axis, which the off-axis eye cameras never gave for free (that
             mismatch -- x_norm sitting at +0.20 to +0.29, not 0, at the real
             target distance -- was the whole source of the earlier tuning
             grief; see elephant_grasp_cv.py).

             Height (Z) was NOT a free choice -- the robot's own trunk hangs/
             curls UP in front of the face by default (no cable tension) and
             completely fills a face-LEVEL camera's frame; confirmed directly
             by rendering and looking (saved test images), not assumed. A
             first pass shipped this at z=0.45 local to clear that -- correct
             functionally, but it read as a periscope towering over the
             robot's back, not "mounted on the face" (direct user pushback:
             "why is the camera all up there... it should be mounted to the
             face"). Re-tested with real WALKING runs (a static teleport test
             at fixed poses is a poor proxy here -- gait bob/pitch changes
             the trunk/camera geometry frame to frame in a way a frozen pose
             doesn't capture) at several lower heights: z=0.18 local (barely
             above the head box's own top, genuinely face-level, not a mast)
             gave 100% detection across a full logged approach (266/266
             camera frames, ox 0.58 down to ~0) with steering about as
             well-centred as the z=0.45 version -- z=0.12 (fully inside the
             head box) also mostly worked (96%) but steering got
             significantly worse at close range (x_norm drifting to -0.55+),
             so z=0.18 is the shipped value: the lowest, most face-anchored
             height tested that didn't cost real accuracy. Tilt and xyaxes
             computed with the exact same _cam_axes formula
             build_elephant_quadruped.py uses for eye_left/eye_right, at
             yaw=0 (no vergence -- there is only one of these, looking
             straight down the trunk's own axis, not two eyes fixating a
             shared point). -->
        <camera name="trunk_cam" pos="0.0900 0.0000 0.1800" xyaxes="0 -1 0 0.6428 0 0.7660"/>
        <geom name="trunk_cam_marker" type="sphere" pos="0.0900 0.0000 0.1800" size="0.014" material="eye_mat" contype="0" conaffinity="0"/>
